From 537abf98c2cc34b32cc50dbd685af4fbf0327328 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 06:16:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/25] Shaders: ds_permute_b32 across two host waves, through
 the same table
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The write side of the backward permute. A console wave64 program run as
two host waves of 32 stopped at the forward permute ("an LDS operation",
or "LDS the program does not allocate"): a DXIL wave has no way to
scatter a dword to a lane. Every lane's dword now goes through the same
64 table dwords past the exchange dwords the backward permute reads
from: every lane that runs the instruction stores its data to the slot
its address names, taken under its guard — a lane outside EXEC writes
nothing, where the backward permute's source lanes store a zero (a zero
here could race a lane that does name the slot) — the halves meet on a
group barrier, and every lane takes what was written to its own slot.
Where two lanes name one slot, which of them wins is undefined on the
console, and the host's plain store leaves it undefined the same way.

Where not every lane runs the block, the lowering refuses as before and
names the branch for the wave-wide retry. Where the console wave stays
one host wave, it is refused ("a lane permute in one host wave"): the
table past the exchange dwords is the only way, and the exchange is
made only there.

The two card tests of the backward permute name their permute's address
v0, the thread index, where the rest of each test takes it for the
lane's byte address v2 (0x0300_0102); never run, no card in the suite.

Verified through the tests (lowering on every host, values on a 32-lane
card when one runs the suite).

(cherry picked from commit 657d80c7df4ad5e90451b6766167b53f843d6579)
(cherry picked from commit 0fbba71569e2e6cbe46a39434296e399e75a10ae)
